Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• "grant_date": filter patents granted between 20100101 – 20231231  
• "country_code": filter to Chinese (CN) patents only  
• "family_id": identify the patent family each record belongs to  
• "application_number": count how many distinct applications exist inside each family  
• "publication_number": convenient for quick visual checks (not strictly needed for the final count)  

Typical conditions that may be used  
• "grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20231231  
• "country_code" = 'CN'  
• HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT "application_number") > 1 to keep only families with more than one distinct application  

Below are ten Snowflake SQL queries, moving from quick exploratory checks to the final answer.  
All column names and table names are wrapped in double‑quotes and each query is limited to 20 rows where sensible.

```sql
-- 1. Description: Peek at any Chinese records to understand column values.
SELECT 
  "publication_number",
  "kind_code",
  "grant_date",
  "country_code",
  "family_id",
  "application_number"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS
WHERE "country_code" = 'CN'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: List distinct grant years for Chinese patents.
SELECT 
  DISTINCT FLOOR("grant_date" / 10000) AS "grant_year"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS
WHERE "country_code" = 'CN'
  AND "grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20231231
ORDER BY "grant_year"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Show the 20 families with the largest number of distinct applications (CN, 2010‑2023).
SELECT 
  "family_id",
  COUNT(DISTINCT "application_number") AS "distinct_apps"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS
WHERE "country_code" = 'CN'
  AND "grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20231231
GROUP BY "family_id"
ORDER BY "distinct_apps" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Sample one family that exceeds one application to verify data.
SELECT 
  "family_id",
  "publication_number",
  "application_number",
  "grant_date"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS
WHERE "country_code" = 'CN'
  AND "grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20231231
  AND "family_id" IN (
        SELECT "family_id"
        FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS
        WHERE "country_code" = 'CN'
          AND "grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20231231
        GROUP BY "family_id"
        HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT "application_number") > 1
      )
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Count how many CN patents (records) were granted 2010‑2023, regardless of family size.
SELECT 
  COUNT(*) AS "total_cn_grants_2010_2023"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS
WHERE "country_code" = 'CN'
  AND "grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20231231;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Count families that have >1 applications (CN, 2010‑2023).
SELECT 
  COUNT(*) AS "families_with_multiple_apps"
FROM (
  SELECT 
    "family_id"
  FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS
  WHERE "country_code" = 'CN'
    AND "grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20231231
  GROUP BY "family_id"
  HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT "application_number") > 1
) f;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Using a CTE to mark multi‑application families, then list 20 of their publications.
WITH multi_app_families AS (
  SELECT 
    "family_id"
  FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS
  WHERE "country_code" = 'CN'
    AND "grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20231231
  GROUP BY "family_id"
  HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT "application_number") > 1
)
SELECT 
  p."publication_number",
  p."family_id",
  p."application_number"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p
JOIN multi_app_families m
  ON p."family_id" = m."family_id"
WHERE p."country_code" = 'CN'
  AND p."grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20231231
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Directly count CN patents between 2010‑2023 that sit in a multi‑application family.
SELECT 
  COUNT(*) AS "cn_patents_in_multi_app_families"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p
WHERE p."country_code" = 'CN'
  AND p."grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20231231
  AND p."family_id" IN (
        SELECT "family_id"
        FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS
        WHERE "country_code" = 'CN'
          AND "grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20231231
        GROUP BY "family_id"
        HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT "application_number") > 1
      );
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Same as #8 but returns the breakdown by year.
SELECT 
  FLOOR(p."grant_date" / 10000) AS "grant_year",
  COUNT(*) AS "patent_count"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p
WHERE p."country_code" = 'CN'
  AND p."grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20231231
  AND p."family_id" IN (
        SELECT "family_id"
        FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS
        WHERE "country_code" = 'CN'
          AND "grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20231231
        GROUP BY "family_id"
        HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT "application_number") > 1
      )
GROUP BY "grant_year"
ORDER BY "grant_year"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: Final answer – total number of CN patents granted 2010‑2023 whose family owns >1 distinct applications.
SELECT 
  COUNT(*) AS "cn_granted_2010_2023_in_multi_app_families"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p
WHERE p."country_code" = 'CN'
  AND p."grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20231231
  AND p."family_id" IN (
        SELECT "family_id"
        FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS
        WHERE "country_code" = 'CN'
          AND "grant_date" BETWEEN 20100101 AND 20231231
        GROUP BY "family_id"
        HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT "application_number") > 1
      );
```
